readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|God shall ''wipe away'' all tears from their eyes.
{{right|Rev. xxi. 4.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Sword, I will hallow thee for this thy deed, . . .
Ne’er shall this blood be ''wiped from'' thy point.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iv. 10. 74.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Why, then, should I now, now when glorious peace
Triumphs in change of pleasures, be ''wip’d off'',
Like a useless moth, from courtly ease?</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', Love’s Sacrifice, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Oh, thou has nam’d a word that ''wipes away''
All thoughts revengeful.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid’s Tragedy, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Yet here hee smoothly seeks to ''wipe off'' all the envy of his evill Government upon his Substitutes and under Officers. {{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, i.}}}}

'''3.''' Figuratively, to cleanse, as from evil practices or abuses; clear, as of disadvantage or superfluity.
{{smaller block|I will ''wipe'' Jerusalem as a man wipeth a dish.
{{right|2 Ki. xxi. 13.}}}}
'''4†.''' To cheat; defraud; trick.
{{smaller block|If they by covin or guile be ''wiped'' beside their goods, so that no violence be done to their bodies, they ease their anger by abstaining from occupying with that nation until they have made satisfaction.
{{right|''Sir T. More'', Utopia (tr. by Robinson), ii. 10.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>We are but quit; you fool us of our moneys
In every cause, in every quiddit ''wipe'' us.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Spanish Curate, v. 5.}}}}
'''5†.''' To stroke or strike gently; tap.
{{smaller block|Thenne he toke me by the hande frome the grounde and ''wyped'' my face with a rose and kyssed me.
{{right|''Joseph of Arimathie'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 30.}}}}

readings.c1s4:
¶
{{smaller block|He comes full upon it, seated upright, with its back against a tree, ''wiping'' at the dogs swarming upon it, right and left, with its huge paws.
{{right|''W. M. Baker'', New Timothy, p. 205.}}}}

'''wipe<sup>1</sup>''' (wīp), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''wype''; < ''wipe''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' The act or process of wiping clean or dry; a sweeping stroke of one thing over another; a rub; a brush.
{{smaller block|He often said of himself, with a melancholy ''wipe'' of his sleeve across his brow, that he “didn’t know which-a-way to turn.”
{{right|''George Eliot'', Felix Holt, viii.}}}}
'''2.''' A quick or hard stroke; a blow, literally or figuratively; a cut: now regarded as slang.
{{smaller block|Since you were the first that layde hand to weapon, the fault is not mine if I haue happened to giue you a ''wype''.
{{right|''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 235.}}
<poem>To statesmen would you give a ''wipe'',
You print it in Italic type.</poem>
{{right|''Swift'', On Poetry.}}}}
'''3.''' The mark of a blow or wound; a scar; a brand. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>The blemish that will never be forgot;
Worse than a slavish ''wipe'', or birth-hour’s blot.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 537.}}}}
'''4.''' Something used in wiping; specifically, a handkerchief. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|<poem>I’m Inspector Field!
And this here warment ’s prigged your ''wipe''.</poem>
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, II. 355.}}
“And what have you got, my dear?” said Fagin to Charley Bates. “''Wipes'',” replied Master Bates, at the same time producing four pocket-handkerchiefs.
{{right|''Dickens'', Oliver Twist, ix.}}}}

