'''5.''' ''pl.'' A fence of brushwood. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.] —'''6.''' Same as ''wiper'', 3.
{{smaller block|As the cam, which is a revolving wheel with twelve or fourteen projecting teeth or ''wipes'', revolves.
{{right|''W. H. Greenwood'', Steel and Iron, p. 308.}}}}

¶
'''wipe<sup>2</sup>''' (wīp), ''n.'' Same as ''weep''<sup>2</sup>.

'''wiper''' (wī′pėr), ''n.'' [< ''wipe''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who or that which wipes.
{{smaller block|Another movement [of a soldering-machine] carries the can body across the ''wiper'', which removes the superfluous solder.
{{right|''Sci. Amer.'', N. S., LXIII. 297.}}}}
'''2.''' That on which anything is wiped, as a hand-towel or a handkerchief.
{{smaller block|The ''wipers'' for their noses. ''B. Jonson'', Masque of Owls.}}
'''3.''' In ''mach.'', a piece projecting generally from a horizontal axle, for the purpose of raising stampers, pounders, or pistons in a vertical direction and letting them fall by their own weight. Wipers are employed in fulling-mills, stamping-mills, oil-mills, powder-mills, etc. Also ''wipe''. —'''4.''' A steel implement for cleaning the bore of a musket, etc.
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''a'', wiper; ''b'', toe.}}}}
{{smaller|It has two twisted arms, screws on the end of a ramrod, and carries a piece of cloth or a bunch of tow. The larger wipers for cleaning cannon are attached to a wooden stick, and are termed ''worms'' or ''sponges''. See cut under ''gun''.}}

'''wiper-wheel''' (wī′pėr-hwēl), ''n.'' A cam-wheel serving to lift a trip-hammer, a stamp, or the like, allowing it to fall again by its own weight. See ''cam''<sup>1</sup>.

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''wiping''' (wī′ping), ''n.'' '''1.''' The act of one who wipes; specifically, a beating; a thrashing; a trimming. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|Even in the domestic circle one can have a choice of “a towelling,” “a basting,” “a clouting,” . . . “a trimming,” or “a ''wiping'',” when occasion requires.
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., VII. 153.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''plumbing'': (''a'') The removal, with a greased cloth, of solder which has been poured upon a joint to heat it before soldering. (''b'') The operation of shaping with a wooden pad a mass of solder applied to form a wiped joint.

'''wiping-rod''' (wī′ping-rod), ''n.'' See ''wiper'', 4.

'''wird†''', '''wirde†''', ''n.'' Obsolete variants of ''weird''.

'''wire<sup>1</sup>''' (wīr), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''wir'', ''wyr'', < AS. ''wīr'', a wire, a spiral ornament of wire, = MLG. ''wire'', LG. ''wir'', wire; cf. OHG. ''wiara'', MHG. ''wiere'', fine-drawn gold, gold ornament, = Icel. ''vīrr'', wire (cf. Sw. ''vire'', wind, twist); cf. Lith. ''wela'', iron wire, L. ''viriæ'', armlets (see ''virole'', ''ferrule'').] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' An extremely elongated body of elastic material; specifically, a slender bar of metal, commonly circular in section, from the size which can be bent by the hand with some difficulty down to a fine thread. {{smaller|Wire was originally made by hammering, a sort of groove in the anvil serving to determine the size. It is now drawn by powerful machinery, and passed through a series of holes constantly diminishing in size. Wire of square section, flat like a tape, etc., is also made.}}
{{smaller block|Fetislich hir fyngres were fretted with golde ''wyre''.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), ii. 11.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Wyre.'' Filum, vel ferrifilum . . . (filum ereum vel ferreum, P.).
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 530.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|At what period and among what people the art of working up pure gold, or gilded silver, into a long, round hair-like thread — into what may be correctly called ''wire'' — began, is quite unknown.
{{right|''S. K. Handbook Textile Fabrics'', p. 22.}}}}

'''2†.''' A twisted thread; a filament.
{{smaller block|<poem>Upon a courser, startling as the fyr,
Men mighte turne him with a litel ''wyr'',
Sit Eneas, lyk Phebus to devyse.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 1205.}}}}

'''3.''' A quantity of wire used for various purposes, especially in electric transmission, as in case of the telephone, the telegraph, electric lighting, etc.; specifically, a telegraph-wire, and hence (colloquially) the telegraph system itself: as, to send orders by ''wire''.
{{smaller block|It is ridiculous to make love by ''wire''.
{{right|''C. D. Warner'', Their Pilgrimage, p. 301.}}}}
{{smaller block|Faraday’s term “electrode,” literally a way for electricity to travel along, might be well applied to designate the insulated conductor along which the electric messenger is despatched. It is, however, more commonly and familiarly called “the ''wire''” or “the line.”
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXIII. 113.}}}}

'''4.''' A metallic string of a musical instrument; hence, poetically, the instrument itself.
{{smaller block|<poem>Sound Lydian ''wires'', once make a pleasing note
On nectar streams of your sweet airs to float.</poem>
{{right|''Marston'', Antonio and Mellida, I., v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Listening to what unshorn Apollo sings
To the touch of golden ''wires''.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Vacation Exercise, l. 38.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>With ''wire'' and catgut he concludes the day,
Quav’ring and semiquav’ring care away.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Progress of Error, l. 126.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
5'''†.''' The lash; the scourge: alluding to the use of metallic whips.
{{smaller block|Thou shalt be whipp’d with ''wire.''
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., ii. 5. 65.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Lol.'' You may hear what time of day it is, the chimes of Bedlam goes.
''Alib.'' Peace, peace, or the ''wire'' comes!</poem>
{{right|''Middleton and Rowley'', Changeling, i. 2.}}}}

'''6.''' In ''ornith.'', one of the extremely long, slender, wire-like filaments or shafts of the plumage of various birds. See ''wired'', ''wire-tailed'', and cut under ''Videstrelda.''—'''7.''' ''pl.'' Figuratively, that by which any organization or body of persons is controlled and directed: now used chiefly in political slang. See ''wire-pulling''.
{{smaller block|Now, however, there was a vacancy, and they [the politicians] scented their prey afar off. The usual manipulation of the ''wires'' began, and they were managed with the usual skill. {{right|''The Nation'', XVI. 330.}}}}

'''8.''' A pickpocket with long fingers, expert at picking women’s pockets. ''Hotten.'' [Thieves’ slang.]
{{smaller block|He was worth 20''l.'' a week, he said, as a ''wire''—that is, a picker of ladies’ pockets.
{{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, I. 410.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller|soaked in water, beaten, and spun into a sort of thread of great strength. These threads are dipped in melted tin, and drawn through a horn with a hole in it. The Laplanders use this wire for embroidering their clothes.—'''Wire-twisting machine''', a machine or tool for joining ends of wire, as sections of fencing- or telegraph-wires etc., by twisting them on each other.—'''Woven-wire lathing.''' See ''lathing''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Made of wire; consisting of or fitted with wires: as, a ''wire'' sieve; a ''wire'' bird-cage.
{{smaller block|<poem>He did him to the ''wire''-window,
As fast as he could gang.</poem>
{{right|''Fire of Frendraught'' (Child’s Ballads, VI. 180).}}}}

{{smaller|'''Wire armor.''' Same as ''chain-mail.'' See ''mail''<sup>1</sup>, 3.—'''Wire belting''', belts or straps for machinery, made of wire instead of leather.—'''Wire bent.''' See ''bent''<sup>2</sup>.—'''Wire bridge.''' (''a'') Same as ''suspension-bridge.'' See ''bridge''<sup>1</sup> (with cut). (''b'') In ''elect.'', a kind of Wheatstone bridge in which two adjacent resistances are formed by a wire which can be divided in any ratio by means of a sliding contact and a graduated scale.—'''Wire cables.''' See ''cable.''—'''Wire cartridge''', a cartridge for a shotgun, having the charge of shot inclosed in a network of wire to concentrate the discharge.}}
{{smaller block|''Wire cartridges'' are woven wire receptacles in which shot are mixed with bone dust. {{right|''Sportsman’s Gazetteer'', p. 568.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Wire cloth.''' See ''cloth.''—'''Wire entanglements''', in ''fort.'' See ''entanglement.''—'''Wire fence, gauze, guard, gun.''' See the nouns.—'''Wire mattress.''' See ''mattress.''—'''Wire rope.''' See ''rope''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Wire-spring coiling-machine''', a machine for making spiral metal springs.—'''Wire stitch.''' See ''stitch'', 9.—'''Wire wheel.''' See ''wheel''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''wire'''<sup>1</sup> (wīr), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''wired'', ppr. ''wiring.'' [< ''wire''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To bind, fit, or otherwise provide with wire; put wire in, on, around, through, etc.: as, to ''wire'' corks in bottling liquors; to ''wire'' beads; to ''wire'' a fence; to ''wire'' a bird-skin, as in taxidermy; to ''wire'' a house for electric lighting.

readings.c3s4:
{{smaller block|<poem>As bats at the ''wired'' window of a dairy,
They beat their vans.</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Witch of Atlas, xvi.}}}}
{{smaller block|In 1711 the coats used to be ''wired'' to make them stick out. ''J. Ashton'', Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, I. 151.}}
{{smaller block|Many of the houses built during the past two years were ''wired'' when constructed.
{{right|''Electric Rev.'' (Amer.), XV. 4.}}}}

'''2.''' To snare by means of a wire: as, to ''wire'' a bird.
{{smaller block|<poem>Donald Caird can ''wire'' a maukin,
Kens the wiles o’ dun-deer staukin’.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Donald Caird’s Come Again.}}}}
